# PCB Design Glossary

Key terms and definitions used across PCB layout, signal integrity, fabrication, and assembly — explained by Qmax Systems engineers.

## Activating
The process of preparing a non-conductive surface for electroless plating by first treating it with a catalyst.

## Active component
A component that requires power to operate and can amplify or switch signals, such as transistors, ICs, and diodes.

## Additive Process
A method of PCB fabrication where the conductive pattern is built up on the base material by plating, rather than etching away unwanted copper.

## AlN
Aluminum Nitride. A ceramic material with high thermal conductivity used as a substrate for high-power electronic components.

## ALN Substrate
A substrate made from aluminum nitride, used in high-power and high-frequency applications due to its excellent thermal conductivity and electrical insulation.

## Air gap
The space between conductive elements on a PCB, critical for electrical isolation and preventing arcing.

## Alumina
Aluminum oxide (Al2O3), a ceramic material commonly used as a substrate in hybrid circuits and for its electrical insulation properties.

## Ambient
Refers to the surrounding environment, particularly the temperature surrounding a component or PCB, which affects heat dissipation and component performance.

## Analog Circuit
A circuit that processes continuously variable signals, as opposed to discrete digital signals.

## Aperture
An opening in a stencil or artwork through which solder paste is deposited onto a PCB pad.

## Aperture wheel
A rotating disk with multiple aperture openings used in photo-plotters to create different line widths and shapes on PCB artwork.

## Aperture Information
Data that defines the size, shape, and type of each aperture used in a Gerber file for PCB artwork generation.

## ApertureList/ApertureTable
A list or table in a Gerber file that defines all the apertures (shapes and sizes) used in the PCB artwork.

## AOI
Automated Optical Inspection. A system that uses cameras and image processing to automatically inspect PCBs for defects after manufacturing.

## AQL
Acceptable Quality Level. A statistical quality standard that defines the maximum number of defective units in a sample that is considered acceptable.

## Array (PCB layout)
An arrangement of multiple identical PCB designs on a single panel for efficient manufacturing.

## Array (components)
A group of components arranged in a regular pattern, often in a single package.

## Artwork Master
The original, high-precision film or digital file used to create the photomasks for PCB fabrication.

## Aspect Ratio
The ratio of the depth (thickness) of a drilled hole to its diameter. A high aspect ratio makes plating more difficult.

## Artwork
The pattern of conductors, pads, and other features on a PCB layer, represented as a Gerber or other CAD file.

## ASCII
American Standard Code for Information Interchange. A character encoding standard used in data files, including some PCB file formats.

## Assembly
The process of mounting and soldering electronic components onto a PCB.

## Assembly drawing
A document that shows the placement and orientation of all components on a PCB, used as a guide for assembly.

## Assembly house
A facility that specializes in mounting and soldering components onto PCBs (also called a Contract Manufacturer or CM).

## ASTM
American Society for Testing and Materials. An organization that develops and publishes technical standards, including standards for PCB materials and testing.

## AWG
American Wire Gauge. A standard for wire diameter in the US.

## Automated Test Equipment (ATE)
Equipment used to automatically test the functionality and quality of PCBs or electronic assemblies.

## Automatic component placement
The use of automated machines (pick-and-place machines) to mount components onto a PCB.

## Automatic optical component inspection
See AOI.

## Automatic X-Ray component/pin inspection
A quality inspection method using X-rays to detect hidden defects such as solder joint quality under BGA packages.

## Autorouter
Software that automatically routes PCB traces based on the netlist and design rules.

## BareBoard
A PCB that has been fabricated but has no components mounted on it yet.

## BaseLaminate
The core insulating material of a PCB before any copper or other layers are added.

## Buried via
A via that connects only internal layers of a multilayer PCB and does not extend to the outer surfaces.

## Built in Self Test
A design technique where a circuit tests itself automatically, typically upon power-up or on command.

## B:Stage
A stage in the curing of thermosetting resins where the material is partially cured. The material is still flexible and can be further processed.

## Barrel
The plated hole in a PCB that connects copper layers. The barrel is the cylindrical wall of copper that forms a via or through-hole.

## Base Material
The insulating substrate of a PCB, onto which copper foil is laminated. Common base materials include FR-4 and polyimide.

## Base Material Thickness
The thickness of the insulating core of a PCB, measured without the copper foil.

## Bed of Nails
A test fixture with many spring-loaded pins (nails) that make electrical contact with test points on a PCB for in-circuit testing.

## Blister
A delamination defect where a bubble forms between layers of a PCB due to entrapped moisture or other contamination.

## Board House
A facility that manufactures PCBs (also called a PCB fabricator).

## Board Thickness
The total thickness of a finished PCB, including all layers and the copper foil.

## Book
A stack of multiple PCB panels pressed together for lamination.

## Bond Strength
The force required to separate two bonded materials, such as copper foil from the PCB base material.

## Bow
A deviation of a PCB from a flat plane, measured as the maximum deflection from a flat reference surface.

## Border Area
The area around the edge of a PCB panel that is used for tooling, registration, and handling, and is typically discarded after routing.

## Burr
A rough edge or protrusion left on a drilled hole or machined edge on a PCB.

## Ball grid array (BGA)
A type of surface mount package where the solder connections are a grid of solder balls on the bottom of the package.

## Base
The lowest layer in a stack or structure, or the base material of a PCB.

## Beam lead
A thin metal lead that extends from an IC chip, used in some older packaging technologies.

## Board
A printed circuit board (PCB).

## Body
The main plastic or ceramic housing of a component, excluding its leads.

## Boundary Scan test
A method of testing ICs and PCBs using a serial test access port (JTAG). Test data is shifted in and out through the boundary scan cells in each IC.

## Base Copper
The copper foil that is laminated to the base material of a PCB before any plating or etching.

## Blind Via
A via that connects an outer layer of a PCB to one or more inner layers, but does not pass through the entire board.

## B:Stage Material
See B:Stage.

## B:Stage Resin
The partially cured resin in prepreg material.

## Buildtime
The time required to fabricate a PCB or assembly.

## CAD
Computer-Aided Design. Software used to design PCBs and other electronic products.

## CAM
Computer-Aided Manufacturing. The use of computer software to control manufacturing equipment for PCB fabrication.

## CAM Files
The set of files (Gerber, drill, fabrication drawing, etc.) required by a PCB manufacturer to fabricate a PCB.

## Chamfer
A beveled edge on a PCB component lead or board edge connector, used to ease insertion.

## Card
An informal term for a printed circuit board.

## Capacitance
The ability of a conductor or component to store electrical charge. Parasitic capacitance in PCBs can affect signal integrity.

## Catalyst
A chemical agent that activates a non-conductive PCB surface so that it can be plated with copper in the electroless plating process.

## Ceramic Ball Grid Array (CBGA)
A BGA package that uses a ceramic substrate instead of a plastic substrate, offering better electrical and thermal performance.

## CEM1orCEM3
Composite Epoxy Material grades 1 and 3. Alternative PCB base materials to FR-4, using a composite of epoxy resin and glass or paper.

## Center to Center Spacing
The distance between the centers of adjacent pads, holes, or components. Also called pitch.

## Check Plots
Printed or on-screen representations of CAD data used to verify the design before manufacture.

## Chip on Board (COB)
A packaging technology where a bare semiconductor chip is mounted directly on a PCB and wire-bonded, then encapsulated.

## Check plots
Plots generated from CAD data and checked by the PCB designer to verify the design before sending to manufacturing.

## Chip
A miniaturized electronic circuit or component made from semiconductor material, typically silicon.

## Chip scale package
A very small IC package where the package size is nearly the same as the die size.

## Circuit
An interconnection of electronic components through which electrical current can flow to perform a function.

## Circuit Board
See Printed Circuit Board.

## CIM
Computer-Integrated Manufacturing. The use of computers to control the entire manufacturing process.

## Circuitry Layer
A layer in a PCB that contains traces, pads, or other conductive patterns.

## Clad
A base material to which copper foil has been laminated. Copper clad laminate is the starting material for PCB manufacturing.

## Clearances
The minimum distances required between conductive elements on a PCB to prevent short circuits and ensure electrical isolation.

## Clearance Hole
A non-plated hole in a PCB that provides physical clearance for a component lead or fastener without making an electrical connection.

## CNC
Computer Numerical Control. The use of computers to control machine tools, such as PCB drills and routers.

## Component
An individual electronic part (resistor, capacitor, IC, etc.) that is mounted on a PCB.

## Component Hole
A hole in a PCB through which a component lead is inserted for through-hole mounting.

## Component Side
The side of a PCB on which most components are mounted. Also called the top side or solder side for SMD components.

## Conductive Pattern
The complete arrangement of conductors, pads, vias, and other conductive features on a PCB layer.

## Conductor Spacing
The minimum distance between two adjacent conductors on a PCB.

## Continuity
The presence of an electrical connection between two points. Tested using a continuity tester.

## Conformal Coating
A thin protective coating applied to a PCB assembly to protect it from moisture, dust, and other environmental hazards.

## Connection
An electrical link between two or more points in a circuit.

## Connectivity
The network of electrical connections in a circuit.

## Connector
An electromechanical component that provides a separable electrical connection between a PCB and other components or cables.

## Connector Area
The area on a PCB dedicated to connector placement and routing.

## Controlled Impedance
PCB traces whose impedance is maintained within specified tolerances through careful control of trace geometry and dielectric properties.

## Core Thickness
The thickness of the insulating core material of a multilayer PCB, between the copper foil layers.

## Core Thickness (laminate)
The overall thickness of a copper clad laminate, including the base material and copper foil.

## Coating
A material applied to the surface of a PCB or component to protect it or modify its properties.

## Coefficient of Thermal Expansion (CTE)
The rate at which a material expands or contracts per unit of temperature change.

## Contact Angle (Wetting Angle)
The angle formed between a liquid solder and the surface it contacts, which indicates the quality of wetting.

## Copper Foil (Base Copper Weight)
The thickness of the copper foil on a PCB, typically specified in ounces per square foot.

## Copper Foil
A thin sheet of copper that is laminated to the base material of a PCB.

## Control Code
A special character or command used in machine control languages such as Gerber.

## Corrosive Flux
A flux that contains acid or other corrosive agents. Must be thoroughly cleaned after soldering.

## Crosshatching
A pattern of crossed conductor lines used to fill copper areas on inner layers of multilayer PCBs to reduce the amount of copper and control thermal gradients.

## Curing
The process of hardening a polymer material through chemical reaction, typically induced by heat or UV radiation.

## Curing Time
The time required for a material to fully cure.

## Cutlines
Lines on a PCB panel that indicate where the individual boards are to be separated.

## Database
In PCB design, the CAD database contains all the design data for a PCB, including schematic, layout, BOM, and other information.

## Date Code
A code on a component that indicates the date of manufacture.

## Datum
A reference point, line, or plane from which measurements are taken on a PCB.

## Delamination
Separation of the layers of a PCB, often caused by moisture, thermal stress, or contamination.

## Design Rule Checking
An automated check performed by CAD software to verify that a PCB design conforms to specified design rules (minimum trace width, spacing, etc.).

## Desmear
The process of removing the smear of epoxy resin that can coat the inside of drilled holes, which would prevent good plating adhesion.

## Dewetting
A soldering defect where the molten solder initially wets a surface but then pulls back, leaving a rough, grainy surface.

## DFSM
Dry Film Solder Mask. A photoimageable film laminated to the PCB surface and patterned to protect copper areas from solder.

## Die
A single, unpackaged semiconductor chip.

## Die Bonder
A machine that attaches semiconductor dice to substrates or lead frames in chip-on-board or similar packaging.

## Die Bonding
The process of attaching a semiconductor die to a substrate.

## Dimensional Stability
The ability of a PCB material to maintain its dimensions under changes in temperature and humidity.

## Dimensioned Hole
A hole on a PCB for which the size and location are specifically controlled, often for mechanical or connector mounting purposes.

## Double Side PCB
A PCB with copper traces on both the top and bottom surfaces.

## Double side component assembly
Mounting components on both sides of a PCB.

## Drill Tool Description
The specification of drill bit sizes and types used to drill holes in a PCB.

## Drill File
A file in Excellon or similar format that contains the coordinates and sizes of all holes to be drilled in a PCB.

## Dry Film Resists
A photosensitive film used in PCB fabrication to define patterns for etching or plating.

## Dry Film Solder Mask
See DFSM.

## Edge Connector
A connector formed by extending the copper pads on a PCB to the edge of the board, allowing it to mate with a card-edge connector.

## Edge Clearance
The minimum distance from any conductive feature to the edge of a PCB.

## Electrode deposition
The process of depositing metal onto a surface using electrolysis.

## Electroless Desposition/Plating
The deposition of metal onto a surface without using external electricity, driven by a chemical reduction reaction.

## Electroplating
The process of depositing a metal coating onto a conductive surface using electrolytic action.

## Electrical Test
Testing of a bare PCB to verify that all intended connections are present and no unintended connections exist.

## End-to-end design
A PCB design service that covers all aspects from concept to manufacturing-ready files.

## E-pad
An exposed metallic pad on the bottom of some IC packages (such as QFN) used for thermal dissipation and ground connection.

## Epoxy
A thermosetting polymer material commonly used as the binder in PCB base materials such as FR-4.

## Epoxy Smear
Epoxy resin that is dragged into drilled holes during the drilling process, contaminating the hole walls.

## ESR
Equivalent Series Resistance. The resistance of a capacitor measured at a given frequency, which causes power dissipation and heating.

## Etching
The chemical or electrochemical process of removing unwanted copper from a PCB to create the desired conductor pattern.

## Etch back
The controlled removal of resin from the walls of drilled holes to expose more of the inner layer copper for better plating adhesion.

## Excellon Drill File
A standard file format for specifying drill hole locations, sizes, and types for PCB drilling machines.

## Fab
Short for fabrication. Also refers to the PCB manufacturer.

## Fabrication Drawing
A drawing that provides all the information needed to fabricate a PCB, including board outline, layer stack-up, materials, and special notes.

## Fast Turnaround
PCB prototyping or manufacturing services that deliver boards in a shorter than standard lead time.

## Fiducial Mark
A mark on a PCB used as a reference point by automated assembly equipment to align components accurately.

## Fine Pitch (component)
Describes IC packages with a lead pitch less than 0.65mm, requiring precise assembly.

## Fine Pitch (PCB traces)
Traces with very small width and spacing, requiring advanced fabrication processes.

## Finger
An edge connector contact. See Edge Connector.

## First Article
The first manufactured unit of a design, inspected to verify that it meets the design specifications.

## Files: Gerber
The standard file format for PCB artwork, containing the conductive patterns and other features for each layer.

## Files Ivex
A PCB design software file format.

## Files: Eagle
The file format used by the EAGLE PCB CAD software.

## Files Protel
The file format used by the Protel/Altium Designer PCB CAD software.

## File Submission
The process of sending PCB design files to a manufacturer for quotation or fabrication.

## Finished Copper
The final thickness of copper on a PCB after all plating processes are complete.

## Fine line design
PCB designs with trace widths and spacings smaller than standard, requiring advanced fabrication.

## Flex circuit
A flexible printed circuit board, typically made on a polyimide base material.

## Flux
A chemical agent used during soldering to remove oxides from metal surfaces and improve solder wetting.

## Flying Probe
An electrical test method where movable probes contact PCB test points. Does not require a custom test fixture.

## FR-1
A flame-retardant PCB base material using a paper substrate with phenolic resin binder. Low cost but limited performance.

## FR-2
A flame-retardant PCB base material using a paper substrate with phenolic resin binder, similar to FR-1 but with slightly better electrical properties.

## FR-3
A flame-retardant PCB base material using a paper substrate with epoxy resin binder. Better electrical properties than FR-1 or FR-2.

## FR-4
The most common PCB base material, made of woven fiberglass cloth with an epoxy resin binder. Has good electrical, thermal, and mechanical properties.

## FR-6
A flame-retardant PCB base material using a woven glass fabric with polyester resin.

## Functional Test
Testing of a fully assembled PCB by exercising its intended function, rather than just testing connectivity.

## Gerber Files
See Files: Gerber.

## G10
A non-flame-retardant version of FR-4. Similar properties but without the flame-retardant additives.

## Gerber CAM Viewer
Software used to view and verify Gerber PCB artwork files.

## GI
Glass/Impregnated. A type of PCB base material.

## Glob top
An encapsulation method for bare die or wire bonds, where a bead of epoxy is dispensed over the component to protect it.

## Glue deposit
Adhesive placed on a PCB to hold surface mount components in place before soldering.

## Gold Finger
An edge connector with gold-plated contacts. See Edge Connector.

## Gold plated
A surface finish where gold is plated over the copper pads of a PCB to provide a solderable, corrosion-resistant surface.

## Ground
A common reference point in an electrical circuit, usually at 0 volts.

## Ground Plane
A large area of copper on a PCB layer connected to ground. Provides a low-impedance current return path and shields signals from noise.

## HASL
Hot Air Solder Leveling. A PCB surface finish process where the board is dipped in molten solder and the excess is removed with hot air.

## HDI
High Density Interconnect. A class of PCB technology using fine lines, small vias, and multiple layers to achieve high routing density.

## Hermetic
Refers to a component or package that is completely sealed against moisture and gases.

## Header
A type of connector with multiple pins arranged in a row or grid, typically used for board-to-board or board-to-cable connections.

## Hole Breakout
The extension of a plated hole that breaks through the edge of a pad.

## Hole Density
The number of drilled holes per unit area of a PCB.

## Hole pattern
The arrangement and specifications of all holes on a PCB.

## Imaging
The process of transferring a circuit pattern to a photosensitive material or directly to a PCB surface.

## Immersion Plating
A PCB surface finish process where a thin layer of metal is deposited by chemical displacement, without external electricity.

## Impedance
The total opposition to AC current flow in a circuit, including resistance and reactance.

## Inclusions
Foreign material trapped within the layers of a PCB during manufacturing.

## In-Circuit Test
Testing of individual components on an assembled PCB by contacting each component with a test probe.

## Inkjetting
A PCB manufacturing technique where conductive ink or solder mask is deposited using inkjet printing technology.

## Insulation Resistance
The resistance of the insulating material of a PCB, which should be high to prevent current leakage.

## Inspection Guidelines
Standards and procedures for visually inspecting PCBs and assemblies.

## Internal Layer
A layer within a multilayer PCB that is not on the outer surface. Internal layers may contain power planes, ground planes, or signal traces.

## Internal Power and Ground Layers
Internal layers of a multilayer PCB dedicated to power distribution and ground return.

## Interconnect Stress Test
A test that applies thermal cycling and mechanical stress to a PCB to verify the reliability of its interconnections.

## Interstitial Via Hole
A via that connects non-adjacent internal layers of a multilayer PCB without reaching the outer surfaces.

## Immersion coating
A chemical surface finish applied by immersing the PCB in a plating solution.

## IPC
The Institute for Printed Circuits (now IPC – Association Connecting Electronics Industries). Sets standards for PCB design, fabrication, and assembly.

## Jump Scoring
A V-groove scoring technique where the groove does not extend to the full edge of the panel.

## KGB (Known Good Board)
A verified good PCB used as a reference standard for testing purposes.

## Laminate
The base material of a PCB, consisting of reinforced resin, to which copper foil is bonded.

## Lamination
The process of bonding the layers of a multilayer PCB together using heat and pressure.

## Laminate Thickness
The total thickness of the PCB base material and copper foil.

## Laminate Void
An area within a PCB laminate that lacks resin, potentially compromising structural integrity or insulation.

## Land
A conductive pad or area on a PCB to which a component lead is soldered.

## Laser Photo-Plotter
A machine that uses a laser to expose photosensitive material for PCB artwork generation, offering high resolution and accuracy.

## Layer Sequence
The order of the copper and prepreg layers in a multilayer PCB stackup.

## Layers
The individual copper or insulating planes that make up a multilayer PCB.

## Lay up
The process of arranging the layers of a multilayer PCB stack-up prior to lamination.

## Leakage Current
A small amount of current that flows through an insulator or dielectric, which can be problematic in high-impedance circuits.

## Legend
The text and symbols printed on the surface of a PCB to identify components, test points, and other information. Also called silkscreen.

## Lot
A group of PCBs or components manufactured together under the same conditions.

## Lot Code
A code that identifies the manufacturing lot of a component or PCB.

## LPI
Liquid Photo-Imageable. A type of solder mask that is applied as a liquid and then patterned using photolithography.

## Major Defect
A defect that is likely to cause the PCB or assembly to fail or not meet its specifications.

## Mask
A pattern applied to a PCB to protect certain areas during processing, such as the solder mask.

## Master aperture list
See ApertureList/ApertureTable.

## Measling
A defect in a PCB where the glass weave of the base material is visible as white spots or crosses, caused by moisture, thermal stress, or poor lamination.

## Metal Foil
Thin metal sheet, typically copper, used as the conductive layer in PCBs.

## Micro-Sectioning
A metallographic technique where a small cross-section is cut from a PCB and polished for microscopic examination of its internal structure.

## Microvia
A very small via, typically less than 150 micrometers in diameter, formed by laser drilling. Used in HDI PCBs.

## Mil
One thousandth of an inch (0.001 inch). Commonly used as a unit of measurement in PCB design.

## Minimum Traces & Spacing
The smallest permitted trace width and the minimum spacing between traces, as defined by the PCB manufacturer's capabilities.

## Mounting Hole
A non-plated or plated hole in a PCB used to mechanically fasten the board to a chassis or enclosure.

## Minimum Conductor Width
The smallest allowed trace width on a PCB for a given current capacity and manufacturing process.

## Minimum Conductor Space
The smallest allowed distance between adjacent conductors on a PCB.

## Minor Defect
A defect that does not significantly affect the function or reliability of a PCB or assembly.

## Multilayer PCB
A PCB with more than two layers of conductive copper separated by insulating layers.

## NC Drill
Numerically Controlled Drill. A machine that drills holes in a PCB under computer control.

## NC drill file
See Drill File.

## Negative
A PCB artwork where the image is the opposite of the actual pattern; the exposed areas are removed during processing.

## Net
A set of electrically connected nodes in a schematic or PCB layout.

## Netlist
A list of all the electrical connections in a circuit, defining which component pins are connected to each other.

## Node
A connection point in a circuit where two or more components are connected.

## Notation
Symbols, abbreviations, and conventions used in PCB documentation.

## Nomenclature
The system of names and labels used in PCB design and documentation.

## Notch
A cutout in the edge of a PCB, used for mechanical alignment or to accommodate a specific shape in the chassis.

## NPTH
Non-Plated Through Hole. A drilled hole in a PCB that is not plated with copper, used for mechanical mounting only.

## Number of Holes
The total count of all holes (plated and non-plated) in a PCB.

## Open
An unintended break in a conductor on a PCB, preventing current from flowing where it should.

## OSP
Organic Solderability Preservative. A PCB surface finish where a thin organic film is applied to copper pads to prevent oxidation.

## Outer-Layer
The top or bottom layer of a PCB, which is on the outer surface.

## Pad
A small area of copper on a PCB surface or inner layer to which a component lead is soldered or a via connects.

## Pad annulus
The ring of copper around a drilled hole in a via or through-hole pad.

## Part Number
A unique identifier assigned to a component, PCB, or assembly.

## Panel
A larger sheet of PCB material containing multiple individual PCB designs, used for efficient manufacturing.

## Pattern
The arrangement of conductive and non-conductive features on a PCB layer.

## Pattern Plating
A PCB fabrication process where copper is plated only onto the areas that will become the final circuit pattern.

## PCB
Printed Circuit Board. A board made of insulating material on which conductive copper traces, pads, and other features are formed to connect electronic components.

## PCB database
The complete set of CAD data for a PCB design, stored in a proprietary or standard format.

## PCB-Design-Software/Tools
Software applications used to create PCB layouts, such as Altium Designer, KiCad, Eagle, and OrCAD.

## PCB Fabrication Process
The series of manufacturing steps used to produce a bare PCB.

## PCMCIA
Personal Computer Memory Card International Association. A standard for small expansion cards used in laptops.

## PEC
Printed Electronic Circuit. A circuit formed by printing conductive materials on a substrate.

## Phenolic PCB
A PCB made from a phenolic resin and paper composite base material, such as FR-1 or FR-2.

## Photographic Image
The image created on photosensitive material during PCB artwork generation.

## Photo Print
A print made from the photographic artwork for a PCB.

## Photo-Plotting
The process of creating PCB artwork on photosensitive film or glass using a photo-plotter.

## Photo-Resist
A photosensitive material applied to a PCB surface and used to define the circuit pattern through exposure and development.

## Photo-tool
The photographic film or glass plate containing the PCB artwork used for photolithography.

## Pin
A conductive lead on an electronic component used for soldering or inserting into a socket.

## Pitch
The center-to-center distance between adjacent leads, pads, or holes.

## Pick-and-Place
An automated machine that picks components from tape, tray, or reel feeders and places them on a PCB for soldering.

## PTH
Plated Through Hole. A drilled hole in a PCB that has been plated with copper to provide electrical connection between layers.

## Plating
The process of depositing a thin layer of metal onto a PCB surface or hole wall using electroplating or electroless plating.

## Plating Void
An area within a plated through-hole where the plating is absent, which can compromise the electrical connection.

## Plastic Leaded Chip Carrier (PLCC)
A type of surface mount IC package with J-shaped leads on all four sides.

## Plating Resist
A material applied to areas of a PCB that should not be plated during the plating process.

## Plots
Printed or digital representations of PCB layers used for verification and documentation.

## Positive
A PCB artwork where the image represents the actual conductor pattern.

## Prepreg
Pre-impregnated glass fabric, partially cured, used as the insulating adhesive layer between copper layers in a multilayer PCB.

## Platen
The flat surface of a press used for laminating multilayer PCBs.

## Prototype
An early sample of a PCB used for design verification before full production.

## Quantity
The number of PCBs or assemblies to be produced.

## QFP
Quad Flat Package. A surface mount IC package with leads on all four sides.

## Ratsnest
In PCB design software, the display of unrouted connections as thin lines between pads, showing the connections that still need to be routed.

## Reference Designator
A unique identifier (e.g., R1, C2, U3) assigned to each component on a PCB.

## Reference Dimension
A dimension on a PCB drawing that is given for information only and does not control manufacturing.

## Registration
The alignment of different layers or artwork in a multilayer PCB. Poor registration can cause misalignment of vias and features.

## Readme File
A text file accompanying PCB design files that provides important information about the design or file set.

## Reflow oven
A conveyor oven that uses controlled temperature profiles to melt and solidify solder paste to attach SMD components to a PCB.

## Reflow Soldering
A soldering process in which solder paste is melted and cooled to form solder joints, typically using a reflow oven.

## Resist
A material that protects certain areas of a PCB from etching, plating, or soldering. See Photo-Resist, Solder Mask.

## Resin (Epoxy) Smear
See Epoxy Smear.

## Rigid-flex
A PCB that combines rigid and flexible sections, allowing three-dimensional configurations.

## Revision
A version number or letter indicating the state of a PCB design.

## RF (radio frequency) and wireless design
PCB design considerations specific to high-frequency radio frequency circuits, including impedance control and antenna design.

## RoHS
Restriction of Hazardous Substances. A European Union directive that restricts the use of certain hazardous substances in electrical and electronic equipment, including lead solder.

## RoHS Compliant PCB
A PCB manufactured using materials and processes that comply with the RoHS directive, particularly using lead-free solder.

## Route (or Track)
A copper trace on a PCB that connects two or more pads.

## Router
A machine that cuts the outline of individual PCBs from a panel. Also a software tool that connects PCB pads with traces.

## Schematic
A diagram that shows the electrical connections between components in a circuit, without regard to their physical placement.

## Scoring
A method of creating a V-groove in a PCB panel to allow easy separation into individual boards.

## Screen Printing
The process of printing solder paste or adhesive through a stencil onto a PCB.

## Short: Short circuit
An unintended low-resistance connection between two conductors that should be electrically isolated.

## Short run
A small manufacturing run, typically for prototypes or low-volume production.

## Silk Screen (Silk Legend)
See Legend.

## Sieber Meyer
A company that manufactures PCB handling and processing equipment.

## Single Side PCB
A PCB with copper traces on only one side.

## Single track
A routing strategy where only one trace is routed between through-hole component pins or pads.

## Small Outline Integrated Circuit (SOIC)
A type of surface mount IC package that is smaller than the standard DIP package.

## Size X & Y
The X and Y dimensions of a PCB outline.

## SMOBC
Solder Mask Over Bare Copper. A process where solder mask is applied directly over bare copper, without a surface finish under the mask.

## SMD
Surface Mount Device. A component designed to be mounted on the surface of a PCB, rather than inserted through holes.

## SMT
Surface Mount Technology. The assembly process where SMD components are mounted on the surface of a PCB.

## Solder Bridging
A soldering defect where solder connects two or more conductors that should be electrically isolated.

## Solder Bumps
Small balls of solder attached to the pads of a BGA or flip-chip package.

## Solder Coat
A layer of solder applied to PCB pads as a surface finish. See HASL.

## Solder Leveling
See HASL.

## Solder Mask or Solder resist
A polymer coating applied to a PCB to protect copper traces and prevent solder from bridging between pads during assembly.

## Solder Mask
See Solder Mask or Solder resist.

## Solder Mask (Artwork)
The CAD artwork that defines the openings in the solder mask for component pads.

## Solder Mask Color
The color of the solder mask coating, typically green, but also available in red, blue, black, white, and other colors.

## Solder paste
A mixture of solder particles and flux in a paste form, applied to PCB pads before component placement for reflow soldering.

## Solder Wick
A braided copper wire used to remove excess solder from PCB pads or components.

## SPC
Statistical Process Control. A method of monitoring and controlling a manufacturing process using statistical methods.

## Step-and-Repeat
A process where the same PCB design is reproduced multiple times on a panel to create an array.

## Stuff
An informal term for the process of mounting components onto a PCB.

## Sub-Panel
A smaller panel that is made up from a larger panel during PCB manufacturing.

## Substrate
The base material on which a PCB or hybrid circuit is built.

## Surface Mount
See SMT.

## Surface finish
The coating applied to the copper pads of a PCB to protect them from oxidation and ensure solderability. Common finishes include HASL, OSP, ENIG, and immersion silver.

## TAB
Tape Automated Bonding. A packaging technology where IC chips are bonded to a flexible tape for mounting.

## Tab Routing
A method of connecting individual PCBs in a panel using small tabs that are later broken or routed away.

## Temperature Coefficient (TC)
The rate of change of a material property (such as resistance or capacitance) per unit of temperature change.

## Tented Via
A via whose hole is completely covered by solder mask to prevent solder from entering the hole during assembly.

## Tenting
The process of covering a via with solder mask.

## Terminal
A connection point on a component or PCB for making an electrical connection.

## Test Board
A PCB designed specifically for testing processes, materials, or equipment, rather than for a final product.

## Test Fixture
A custom device used to hold a PCB and make electrical connections to its test points for testing.

## Test Point
A pad or hole on a PCB that provides access for test probes to measure or verify circuit operation.

## Testing
The process of verifying that a PCB or assembly meets its required specifications.

## Test Coupon
A small PCB included in a panel that is used to test the quality of the manufacturing process, such as hole plating quality.

## TG (Tg)
Glass Transition Temperature. The temperature at which a PCB base material changes from a rigid glass-like state to a softer, more rubber-like state. PCBs should not operate above their Tg.

## Thief
Areas of copper added to a PCB pattern to improve plating uniformity by providing additional plating current in areas that would otherwise have uneven plating.

## Through-Hole
A component mounting method where component leads are inserted through holes in the PCB and soldered on the other side.

## Tooling
The equipment, fixtures, and tooling holes used in PCB manufacturing and assembly.

## Tooling Holes
Holes in a PCB or panel used for mechanical alignment and registration in manufacturing equipment.

## Trace/Track
See Route (or Track).

## Traveler
A document that accompanies a PCB or assembly through the manufacturing process, recording the steps completed and any issues found.

## Turnkey
A PCB assembly service where the manufacturer supplies all components and delivers a fully assembled and tested board.

## Twist
A PCB warp where opposite corners of the board are not in the same plane. Measured as the deviation from a flat reference.

## UL
Underwriters Laboratories. An organization that certifies the safety of electronic products and materials.

## Underwriters Symbol
The UL recognition symbol, which indicates that a PCB material has been tested and recognized by UL.

## Unclad
PCB base material without copper foil laminated to it.

## UV Curing
The process of hardening a polymer material using ultraviolet light radiation.

## Valuable Final Artwork
The final, corrected version of the PCB artwork used for manufacturing.

## Via
A plated hole that provides an electrical connection between different layers of a multilayer PCB.

## Void
An area in a solder joint or plated through-hole that lacks solder or plating material.

## V-Scoring
A method of pre-cutting a V-shaped groove in a PCB panel to allow easy separation of individual boards.

## Wave Soldering
A PCB soldering process where the board passes over a wave of molten solder, soldering all through-hole component leads simultaneously.

## Wet solder mask
Liquid solder mask applied by screen printing or spray and then cured. Contrast with dry film solder mask.

## Wicking
The unwanted migration of solder along a conductor or into a plated through-hole by capillary action.

## Wire
A conductor used to make electrical connections. Also refers to a net or trace in PCB design.

## Wire wrap area
An area on a PCB designated for wire-wrap connections, an older prototyping technique.

## X-Axis
The horizontal axis in a Cartesian coordinate system, used to define positions on a PCB.

## Y-Axis
The vertical axis in a Cartesian coordinate system, used to define positions on a PCB.

## Zip File
A compressed archive file format commonly used to bundle and send PCB design files.

## Z-Axis
The axis perpendicular to the surface of a PCB, representing the board thickness direction.
